WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.155
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.155 --> 00:00:03.780
DEBI CABRERA: Do you
use a service mesh

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.340
and worry about compliance?

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.250
Learn how to use a private
certificate authority

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.540
to improve security, strengthen
compliance, and reduce

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.320
headaches.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.530
Today, we'll learn about
Certificate Authority Service,

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.020
its integration with
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.740
and how they work together
to achieve your goals.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.740
Anthos Service Mesh,
or ASM, security

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.020
helps you mitigate
insider threats

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.210
and reduce the risk
of a data breach

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.159
by ensuring that
all communications

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:35.700
between workloads are encrypted,
mutually authenticated,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.150
and authorized, leveraging
industry standards, like Mutual

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.000
TLS, or MTLS for short.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.180
ASM offers a slew of
security benefits.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.630
It mitigates risk of replay
or impersonation attacks

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.890
that use stolen credentials,
ensures encryption in transit,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.070
mitigates the risk of user data
breach within your production

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.730
network by ensuring that only
authorized clients can access

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.670
a service, and identifies
which clients--

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.970
access to service
with sensitive data.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.000
Anthos Service Mesh includes a
Google-managed, multiregional

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.400
private certificate
authority, Mesh CA,

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.160
for issuing
certificates for MTLS.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.280
But what do you do if you
need to back your CA signing

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.630
keys in a Google-managed HSN, or
in a highly regulated industry

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.170
and are subject to compliance,
or need flexibility

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.680
with your on-prem route to move
between clouds without being

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.720
tied to a specific provider?

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.030
Cue Certificate
Authority Service,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.640
which can replace Mesh CA.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.180
CAS is a highly available,
scalable service

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.440
that enables you to simplify,
automate and customize

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.700
the deployment, management, and
security of private Certificate

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.920
Authorities, or CAs.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.700
It allows you to converge all
of your certifications and CAs

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.000
into a single management
pane to reduce your risk

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.870
and increase the control and
visibility over the operations

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.340
in your org.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.070
Do you have a situation
where one team handles

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.490
all the certifications
or where certifications

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.370
can be made without appropriate
visibility for security

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.930
and compliance teams?

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.770
CAS removes the bottlenecks
and increases visibility,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.080
and therefore security,
by providing guardrails.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.150
CAS does this by defining
granular access controls

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.200
and virtual security parameters,
defining per user group

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.560
insurance policy and baseline
certificate extensions,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.350
storing CA keys in Cloud HSM--

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.910
which is FIPS 140-2 Level
3-validated and can help

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.550
achieve various
compliance certifications,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.770
like FedRAMP and SOC--

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.770
and by obtaining logs
and gaining visibility

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:52.080
into who did what, when, and
where with Cloud audit logs.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.150
Additionally, it's
configured by you,

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.900
giving you control over
your infrastructure.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.600
To recap, CAS allows
you to converge

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.700
all of your
certifications and CAs

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.620
into a single management pane.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.960
It reduces your risk
by providing guardrails

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.260
for usage and increases
the control and visibility

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.750
over operations in your
org while simultaneously

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.580
removing bottlenecks, allowing
for increased efficiency.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.970
And you can easily install
it right in the command line.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.970
To install Certificate Authority
Service with Anthos Service

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.450
Mesh, and if you want to learn
how to create a certificate

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.030
authority, check out the
links in the description

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.120
for a helpful video
and documentation.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

